\b YOLO Service SQLAlchemy Refactor Documentation\b0\par
\par
\b 1. Introduction\b0\par
This document explains the SQLAlchemy refactor done in the YOLO service, why it was useful, what changed in the code, and how skills and evals work in this project.\par
\par
\b 2. Why SQLAlchemy is better than using SQLite directly\b0\par
SQLite is a simple embedded database that is good for small projects and local development. SQLAlchemy provides a more structured and scalable way to work with databases.\par
\par
\b Benefits of SQLAlchemy\b0\par
\pard\li720\fi-720\tx720\sa120\sl276\slmult1
\bullet SQLAlchemy uses object-relational mapping (ORM), so database tables are represented as Python classes.\par
\bullet The code becomes cleaner because developers work with Python objects instead of writing many raw SQL queries.\par
\bullet It is easier to maintain because database logic is separated from API logic.\par
\bullet The same code can work with SQLite for development and PostgreSQL for production.\par
\bullet It reduces manual SQL string errors and makes queries more reusable.\par
\bullet Future extensions such as new tables or columns are easier to add.\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1
\par
\b SQLite limitations\b0\par
\pard\li720\fi-720\tx720\sa120\sl276\slmult1
\bullet Raw SQL is often scattered through the application code.\par
\bullet It becomes harder to maintain in larger systems.\par
\bullet Switching databases later is more difficult.\par
\bullet Connections and queries require more manual handling.\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1
\par
\b 3. What changed in the YOLO project\b0\par
The YOLO service was refactored so that the database layer is handled in a cleaner and more professional way.\par
\par
\b Before\b0\par
\pard\li720\fi-720\tx720\sa120\sl276\slmult1
\bullet The application used raw SQLite logic directly inside the API service.\par
\bullet Database operations were mixed with request handling.\par
\bullet The code was less structured.\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1
\par
\b After\b0\par
\pard\li720\fi-720\tx720\sa120\sl276\slmult1
\bullet The database model was moved into SQLAlchemy model classes.\par
\bullet Database sessions were created in a dedicated database module.\par
\bullet FastAPI endpoints now use dependency injection to access the DB session.\par
\bullet The public API behavior stayed the same.\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1
\par
\b 4. Main files changed\b0\par
\b services/yolo/models.py\b0\par
This file defines the database tables as Python classes.\par
\pard\li720\fi-720\tx720\sa120\sl276\slmult1
\bullet PredictionSession stores information about each prediction request.\par
\bullet DetectionObject stores each detected object from the prediction result.\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1
\par
\b services/yolo/db.py\b0\par
This file handles database engine creation, session creation, initialization, and backend selection between SQLite and PostgreSQL.\par
\par
\b services/yolo/app.py\b0\par
The API endpoints were updated to use SQLAlchemy instead of manual SQL statements.\par
\pard\li720\fi-720\tx720\sa120\sl276\slmult1
\bullet Data is inserted using ORM objects.\par
\bullet Data is queried using ORM filters.\par
\bullet The API behavior remains the same.\par
\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1
\par
\b 5. What the refactor achieved\b0\par
The refactor improved the project by separating database logic from business logic, making the code easier to read, and allowing easier future maintenance and database switching.\par
\par
\b 6. What is a skill?\b0\par
A skill is a reusable instruction file that tells an AI coding agent how to perform a specific task correctly.\par
In this project, the skill is stored in the .agents/skills/data-layer/SKILL.md file.\par
\par
\b Purpose of the skill\b0\par
The skill explains when to use the data layer approach, what files to modify, what patterns to follow, what to avoid, and how to preserve existing behavior.\par
\par
\b 7. What are evals?\b0\par
Evals are evaluation cases used to test whether a skill works properly. They are stored in the .agents/skills/data-layer/evals/evals.json file.\par
\par
\b Why evals are important\b0\par
They help ensure that the skill gives the correct guidance, the agent changes the right files, and the implementation follows the expected pattern.\par
\par
\b 8. How skills and evals work together\b0\par
The skill provides instructions for the agent, while the evals check whether the agent followed those instructions correctly. Together, they make the agent more reliable and consistent.\par
\par
\b 9. Summary\b0\par
This assignment showed how to modernize a backend service by moving from direct SQLite handling to SQLAlchemy ORM. The result is a cleaner, more maintainable, and more scalable architecture. Skills and evals help future agents perform similar tasks more reliably.\par
